    "\n",
    "# Describing Data I {.hero #s01-visualisation}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"1\"}\n",
    "Seminar 1 · Data visualisation\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Which Picture for Which Data? {.ojs-slide #chart-choice}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Tick the display that suits each set of data. The reasoning appears underneath.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "// Short labels so all five fit on ONE line at 21px. The fuller names\n",
    "// (stem-and-leaf, pie) live in the reasoning underneath, where there is room.\n",
    "ccOpts = [\"Bar or pie\", \"Histogram\", \"Scatter plot\", \"Time series\", \"Box plot\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "// Exercise 1 of the beamer Seminar 1, answers and reasoning carried over.\n",
    "ccQ = [\n",
    "  {q: \"The tax paid by a light engineering company and its annual profit, for 20 consecutive years.\",\n",
    "   a: \"Scatter plot\",\n",
    "   hint: \"Look again at how many measures there are, and at what pairs them up.\",\n",
    "   why: \"Two numerical measures, and the question is whether they move together. The twenty years supply the pairs, they are not the thing being plotted.\"},\n",
    "  {q: \"The types of premises occupied by all the small businesses in a town: office, factory, warehouse, and so on.\",\n",
    "   a: \"Bar or pie\",\n",
    "   hint: \"Ask first whether any of this can go on a number line at all.\",\n",
    "   why: \"The variable is categorical. There is nothing to put on a number line, only counts in named boxes, so a bar chart or a pie chart.\"},\n",
    "  {q: \"The salary of a trainee accountant in the first year of training, at 30 major accountancy firms.\",\n",
    "   a: \"Histogram\",\n",
    "   hint: \"One measure, thirty values of it, and the shape is what you are after.\",\n",
    "   why: \"One numerical measure, and what you want to see is the shape of its spread. A stem-and-leaf does the same job by hand and keeps the digits.\"},\n",
    "  {q: \"The number of trainee accountants enrolling for professional examinations each year since 1960.\",\n",
    "   a: \"Time series\",\n",
    "   hint: \"There is one measure here, but it is useless without the year beside it.\",\n",
    "   why: \"One measure again, but it only means anything read against the year it belongs to.\"},\n",
    "  {q: \"The years of experience and the current salary of a random sample of economists at a New York bank.\",\n",
    "   a: \"Scatter plot\",\n",
    "   hint: \"Count the measures recorded for each economist.\",\n",
    "   why: \"Two numerical measures per person, so each economist is one point.\"}\n",
    "]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "// What each display is FOR. A wrong answer is explained by naming the job the\n",
    "// chosen chart actually does, which is one line per option rather than one\n",
    "// per (question, wrong option) pair — twenty of those would say the same five\n",
    "// things over and over.\n",
    "ccJob = ({\n",
    "  \"Bar or pie\":   \"A bar or pie chart compares counts across named categories\",\n",
    "  \"Histogram\":    \"A histogram shows the shape of one numerical measure\",\n",
    "  \"Scatter plot\": \"A scatter plot shows how two numerical measures move together\",\n",
    "  \"Time series\":  \"A time series plot follows one measure against time\",\n",
    "  \"Box plot\":     \"A box plot summarises the spread of one numerical measure\"\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "// Every example plot is real worksheet data, so the picture a student sees\n",
    "// here is one they meet again later in the module:\n",
    "//   R&D spend            Seminar 1, Exercise 3   (also slides 8 and 9)\n",
    "//   Executive pay        Seminar 1, Exercise 2\n",
    "//   Executive salaries   Seminar 1, Exercise 4   (the stem-and-leaf one)\n",
    "//   Y against X          Seminar 8, Exercise 2\n",
    "//   Monthly demand       Seminar 9, Exercise 1\n",
    "ccStyle = ({fontSize: \"13px\", fontFamily: \"Roboto, sans-serif\",\n",
    "            color: \"#6E7681\", background: \"transparent\"})\n",
    "\n",
    "ccRD = [6.0, 10.4, 10.5, 9.0, 7.3, 6.6, 6.9, 8.2, 7.1, 8.1,\n",
    "        7.9, 6.8, 7.4, 9.5, 8.1, 13.5, 9.9, 6.9, 11.1, 8.2,\n",
    "        8.0, 7.7, 7.4, 6.5, 9.5, 8.2, 6.9, 7.2, 8.2, 6.7]\n",
    "\n",
    "ccSalary = [846, 457, 563, 1466, 1200, 2164, 746, 1611, 824, 824, 1310,\n",
    "            1007, 1367, 575, 1252, 1354, 2479, 1238, 927, 1253, 925,\n",
    "            1284, 1279, 1660, 860]\n",
    "\n",
    "// Five of the twenty-six, all with SHORT names. The bar chart is the only\n",
    "// plot with words on its y axis, so it used to set the shared left margin at\n",
    "// 88 — which left the other four, and the box plot worst of all, with a band\n",
    "// of empty space down their left side and the drawing pushed off centre.\n",
    "// Picking short names lets every plot share a 44px margin instead.\n",
    "ccPay = [[\"CBS\", 60.25], [\"Exxon\", 34.90], [\"AT&T\", 21.00],\n",
    "         [\"Apple\", 4.17], [\"Tesco\", 1.90]].map(([k, v]) => ({k, v}))\n",
    "\n",
    "ccPairs = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7].map((x, i) => ({x, y: [2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128][i]}))\n",
    "\n",
    "ccMonths = [23, 39, 36, 26, 36, 12, 26, 30, 42, 35, 36, 28]\n",
    "  .map((x, i) => ({month: i + 1, x}))\n",
    "\n",
    "// One geometry for all five: identical margins mean the axes are the same\n",
    "// length and the ticks the same size from plot to plot, so clicking between\n",
    "// displays changes the picture and nothing else. marginLeft is set by the\n",
    "// longest company name on the bar chart; the numeric plots spend that space\n",
    "// on their y-axis label.\n",
    "ccBase = ({\n",
    "  width: 400, height: 152,\n",
    "  marginTop: 10, marginRight: 16, marginBottom: 56, marginLeft: 66,\n",
    "  style: ccStyle\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "// labelOffset 38 against a 48 bottom margin: Plot's default sets the axis\n",
    "// label directly under the tick text and the two touched.\n",
    "// The two labelOffsets are not equal because they are not measured the same\n",
    "// way: Plot pushes the x label DOWN from the axis and the y label LEFT from\n",
    "// it, past tick text of different lengths. These two land both labels the\n",
    "// same distance clear of their tick numbers, which is what reads as even.\n",
    "ccAxX = (label) => ({label, labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 46, tickSize: 5})\n",
    "ccAxY = (label, ticks, domain) => ({label, labelAnchor: \"center\",\n",
    "                                    labelOffset: 55, tickSize: 5,\n",
    "                                    ticks, domain, grid: true})\n",
    "// Explicit tick values, not a count. The shared geometry leaves a 94px plot\n",
    "// area and `ticks: n` is only a HINT — d3 rounds to a nice step, so both 4\n",
    "// and 5 came back with six labels 15px apart under 13px type, all but\n",
    "// touching, while leaving it automatic dropped to two. Naming the values is\n",
    "// the only way to fix the pitch, and these datasets never change.\n",
    "\n",
    "ccDemo = ({\n",
    "  \"Bar or pie\": () => Plot.plot({...ccBase,\n",
    "    x: ccAxX(\"Executive pay ($m)\"),\n",
    "    y: {label: null, tickSize: 5},\n",
    "    marks: [\n",
    "      Plot.barX(ccPay, {y: \"k\", x: \"v\", fill: \"#325D88\",\n",
    "        sort: {y: \"x\", reverse: true}}),\n",
    "      Plot.ruleX([0], {stroke: \"#B0B7C0\"})\n",
    "    ]}),\n",
    "  \"Histogram\": () => Plot.plot({...ccBase,\n",
    "    x: ccAxX(\"R&D spend (% of revenue)\"),\n",
    "    y: ccAxY(\"Firms\", [0, 4, 8]),\n",
    "    marks: [\n",
    "      Plot.rectY(ccRD, Plot.binX({y: \"count\"},\n",
    "        {x: d => d, thresholds: d3.range(6, 15), fill: \"#325D88\"})),\n",
    "      Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: \"#B0B7C0\"})\n",
    "    ]}),\n",
    "  \"Scatter plot\": () => Plot.plot({...ccBase,\n",
    "    x: ccAxX(\"X\"), y: ccAxY(\"Y\", [0, 40, 80, 120]),\n",
    "    marks: [Plot.dot(ccPairs, {x: \"x\", y: \"y\", fill: \"#325D88\", r: 3.4})]}),\n",
    "  \"Time series\": () => Plot.plot({...ccBase,\n",
    "    x: {...ccAxX(\"Month\"), tickFormat: \"d\"}, // From zero: a demand axis that starts at 15 exaggerates every dip.\n",
    "    y: ccAxY(\"Demand\", [0, 25, 50], [0, 50]),\n",
    "    marks: [Plot.line(ccMonths, {x: \"month\", y: \"x\",\n",
    "      stroke: \"#325D88\", strokeWidth: 2})]}),\n",
    "  \"Box plot\": () => Plot.plot({...ccBase,\n",
    "    x: {...ccAxX(\"Executive salary (£000s)\"), grid: true},\n",
    "    y: {label: null, ticks: [], tickSize: 5},\n",
    "    marks: [Plot.boxX(ccSalary, {x: d => d, fill: \"#CBD9E6\", stroke: \"#325D88\"})]})\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "ccIcon = {\n",
    "  const NAVY = \"#325D88\";\n",
    "  const wrap = inner =>\n",
    "    `<svg width=\"42\" height=\"26\" viewBox=\"0 0 42 26\" aria-hidden=\"true\">${inner}</svg>`;\n",
    "  // Bars on a 40-wide strip sitting on a baseline at y = 22. A histogram's\n",
    "  // bars touch and a bar chart's do not, which is the one difference a\n",
    "  // student is meant to read off these thumbnails.\n",
    "  const bars = (hs, pitch, w) => hs.map((h, i) =>\n",
    "    `<rect x=\"${2 + i * pitch}\" y=\"${22 - h}\" width=\"${w}\" height=\"${h}\" fill=\"${NAVY}\"/>`\n",
    "  ).join(\"\");\n",
    "  const line = (x1, y1, x2, y2) =>\n",
    "    `<line x1=\"${x1}\" y1=\"${y1}\" x2=\"${x2}\" y2=\"${y2}\" stroke=\"${NAVY}\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/>`;\n",
    "  return ({\n",
    "    \"Bar or pie\": wrap(bars([16, 9, 13, 6], 10, 7)),\n",
    "    \"Histogram\": wrap(bars([5, 12, 18, 11, 5], 8, 8)),\n",
    "    \"Scatter plot\": wrap([[5,20],[11,16],[16,18],[21,13],[26,11],[32,8],[38,5]]\n",
    "      .map(([x, y]) => `<circle cx=\"${x}\" cy=\"${y}\" r=\"2.1\" fill=\"${NAVY}\"/>`).join(\"\")),\n",
    "    \"Time series\": wrap(\n",
    "      `<polyline points=\"3,20 10,12 17,16 24,7 31,11 39,4\" fill=\"none\"\n",
    "         stroke=\"${NAVY}\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>`),\n",
    "    \"Box plot\": wrap(line(3, 13, 12, 13) + line(28, 13, 39, 13) +\n",
    "      `<rect x=\"12\" y=\"7\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"${NAVY}\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/>` +\n",
    "      line(21, 7, 21, 19))\n",
    "  });\n",
    "}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof ccI = Inputs.radio([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], {value: 1, label: \"Question\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "ccPrompt = html`<div class=\"quiz-q\">${ccQ[ccI - 1].q}</div>`\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof ccPick = {\n",
    "  ccI;   // naming it here rebuilds the input when the question changes, which\n",
    "         // is the only way to clear the previous answer's ticks\n",
    "  // html([...]) rather than html`...`: the sketch is a string of markup and\n",
    "  // the tagged form would escape it into visible angle brackets.\n",
    "  // radio, not checkbox: every question has exactly one right display, so\n",
    "  // letting the room tick three of them only invites a wrong kind of answer.\n",
    "  return Inputs.radio(ccOpts, {\n",
    "    label: \"Suitable display\",\n",
    "    format: v => html([`<span class=\"quiz-opt\">${ccIcon[v]}<span>${v}</span></span>`])\n",
    "  });\n",
    "}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "ccFeedback = {\n",
    "  const want = ccQ[ccI - 1].a;\n",
    "  if (!ccPick) return html`<div class=\"quiz-fb quiz-idle\">Pick a display to see the reasoning.</div>`;\n",
    "  const ok = ccPick === want;\n",
    "  // A wrong pick names the job the chosen chart does and points back at the\n",
    "  // data. It does NOT name the answer: handing it over ends the thinking, and\n",
    "  // the radio is still there to try again with.\n",
    "  const text = ok\n",
    "    ? html`<b>Yes.</b> ${ccQ[ccI - 1].why}`\n",
    "    : html`<b>Not quite.</b> ${ccJob[ccPick]}. ${ccQ[ccI - 1].hint}`;\n",
    "  // The plot always shows the display that was PICKED, not the right one —\n",
    "  // on a wrong answer it is the thing they asked for, which is an argument\n",
    "  // rather than a giveaway.\n",
    "  return html`<div class=\"quiz-answer\">\n",
    "    <div class=\"quiz-fb ${ok ? \"quiz-ok\" : \"quiz-no\"}\">${text}</div>\n",
    "    <figure class=\"quiz-plot\">\n",
    "      <figcaption>${ccPick}</figcaption>\n",
    "      ${ccDemo[ccPick]()}\n",
    "    </figure>\n",
    "  </div>`;\n",
    "}\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
