    "\n",
    "## A Bound that Holds for *Any* Shape {.sim-slide #cheb-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 2 · Chebyshev\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Chebyshev promises at least $1 - 1/k^2$ of any distribution lies within $k$ standard deviations. No normality, no symmetry, no assumptions at all.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "k <- 2 # <-- how many SDs\n",
    "n <- 20000\n",
    "\n",
    "# a wildly skewed population, far from normal\n",
    "x  <- rexp(n, rate = 1) ^ 2\n",
    "mu <- mean(x); s <- sd(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "actual <- mean(abs(x - mu) <= k * s)\n",
    "bound  <- 1 - (1 / k) ^ 2\n",
    "round(c(actual = actual, chebyshev = bound), 3)\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "The bound holds, but loosely — that is the price of assuming nothing. Swap `rexp(n, 1)^2` for `rnorm(n)` and the gap widens further: for a normal, $k = 2$ captures 95%, not the 75% Chebyshev guarantees.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Basic Concepts {.hero #s03-probability}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"3\"}\n",
    "Seminar 3 · Conditional probability and Bayes\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Probability of an Event {.formula-slide #prob-count}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Count how many ways the event can happen, then how many things can happen at all. The probability is the ratio of the two.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) = \\frac{\\#\\ \\text{ways } \\color{#93C54B}{A} \\text{ can happen}}{\\#\\ \\text{of things that can happen}} = \\frac{|\\color{#93C54B}{A}|}{|\\Omega|}$$\n",
    "\n",
    ":::: {.two-col}\n",
    "::: {}\n",
    "[Flip a coin twice]{.col-head}\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\Omega = \\{hh,\\ ht,\\ th,\\ tt\\} \\quad \\color{#6E7681}{\\text{4 in all}}$\n",
    "\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<div class=\"coin-row\">\n",
    "<svg width=\"246\" height=\"34\" viewBox=\"0 0 246 34\" aria-label=\"the four outcomes of two coin flips\">\n",
    "<g font-family=\"Roboto, sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n",
    "<circle cx=\"13\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"rgba(147,197,75,0.18)\" stroke=\"#93C54B\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"13\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#5E8A1E\">H</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"39\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"rgba(147,197,75,0.18)\" stroke=\"#93C54B\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"39\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#5E8A1E\">H</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"75\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"rgba(147,197,75,0.18)\" stroke=\"#93C54B\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#5E8A1E\">H</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"101\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#B0B7C0\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"101\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#6E7681\">T</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"137\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#B0B7C0\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"137\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#6E7681\">T</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"163\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"rgba(147,197,75,0.18)\" stroke=\"#93C54B\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"163\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#5E8A1E\">H</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"199\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#B0B7C0\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"199\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#6E7681\">T</text>\n",
    "<circle cx=\"225\" cy=\"17\" r=\"11\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#B0B7C0\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"225\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#6E7681\">T</text>\n",
    "</g>\n",
    "</svg>\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "[At least one head]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\color{#93C54B}{A} = \\{hh,\\ ht,\\ th\\} \\qquad P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) = \\tfrac{3}{4} = 75\\%$\n",
    "\n",
    "[No heads]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\color{#325D88}{B} = \\{tt\\} \\qquad P(\\color{#325D88}{B}) = \\tfrac{1}{4} = 25\\%$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {}\n",
    "[Roll two dice]{.col-head}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.dice-grid}\n",
    "<div id=\"dicegrid-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\Omega = \\{11,\\ 12,\\ \\ldots,\\ 66\\} \\quad \\color{#6E7681}{\\text{36 in all}}$\n",
    "\n",
    "[At least one five]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\color{#93C54B}{A} = \\{15, 25, 35, 45, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56, 65\\}$\n",
    "\n",
    "$P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) = \\tfrac{11}{36}$\n",
    "\n",
    "[Or split on the first die]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "$P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) = \\tfrac{1}{6} + \\tfrac{5}{6} \\times \\tfrac{1}{6} = \\tfrac{6}{36} + \\tfrac{5}{36} = \\tfrac{11}{36}$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "::::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.pitfall}\n",
    "A tempting answer is 6 + 6 = 12, six rolls with a five on the first die and six with a five on the second. Five and five is in both lists, so twelve counts it twice. The split on the right avoids that, because its second case needs the first die not to be a five.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Sample Spaces and Events {.stage-slide #space-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Roll two dice and every possible result is on the board. The sample space $\\Omega$ is that whole board, and an **event** is any part of it you can point at.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"space-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"space-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"space-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"space-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "When every outcome is equally likely, a probability is a count. $P(\\color{#93C54B}{A})$ is the cells in $\\color{#93C54B}{A}$ over the cells in $\\Omega$. Two fair dice earn that assumption, and most real problems do not.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Unions, Intersections and Complements {.stage-slide #setops-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Two events on the same board. $\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cup \\color{#325D88}{B}$ is \"either\", $\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B}$ is \"both\", and $\\color{#93C54B}{A}^{c}$ is everything left over.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"setops-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"setops-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"setops-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"setops-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Add 6 and 18 and you get 24, which is three too many. The cells that belong to both events were counted twice, so they have to come back off.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Same Picture, Drawn as Circles {.stage-slide #venn-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "A **Venn diagram** is the board with the grid rubbed out. The four counts are the ones you just read off it, and they still add to 36.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"venn-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"venn-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"venn-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"venn-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "These circles are drawn to scale, so $\\color{#325D88}{B}$ really does cover three times the area of $\\color{#93C54B}{A}$. Most are not, which is why the counts written on them matter more than the picture.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Set Notation for Probability {.formula-slide #set-formal}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Counting gives $P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) = |\\color{#93C54B}{A}|/|\\Omega|$ and $P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}^{c}) = 1 - P(\\color{#93C54B}{A})$. Two rules do the rest.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    ":::: {.fragment}\n",
    "::: {.rule-label .rule-or}\n",
    "[Either]{.rw} · $\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cup \\color{#325D88}{B}$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\begin{array}{@{}l@{\\;\\;}c@{\\;\\;}l@{}}\n",
    "P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cup \\color{#325D88}{B}) & = & P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) + P(\\color{#325D88}{B}) - P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B})\\\\[4pt]\n",
    "\\phantom{P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B})} & = & P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) + P(\\color{#325D88}{B}) \\qquad \\color{#6E7681}{\\text{if mutually exclusive}}\n",
    "\\end{array}$$\n",
    "::::\n",
    "\n",
    ":::: {.fragment}\n",
    "::: {.rule-label .rule-and}\n",
    "[Both]{.rw} · $\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B}$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\begin{array}{@{}l@{\\;\\;}c@{\\;\\;}l@{}}\n",
    "P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B}) & = & P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) \\times P(\\color{#325D88}{B} \\mid \\color{#93C54B}{A})\\\\[4pt]\n",
    "\\phantom{P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B})} & = & P(\\color{#93C54B}{A}) \\times P(\\color{#325D88}{B}) \\qquad \\color{#6E7681}{\\text{if independent}}\n",
    "\\end{array}$$\n",
    "::::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "Mutually exclusive means $\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B} = \\varnothing$, so there is nothing to subtract. Independent means learning $\\color{#93C54B}{A}$ tells you nothing about $\\color{#325D88}{B}$, which the next slide makes precise.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Conditional Probabilities\n",
    "\n",
    "## Conditioning Narrows the Sample Space {.stage-slide #cond-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Eight in ten office managers would buy the software ($\\color{#93C54B}{A}$), and four in ten of those would also upgrade ($\\color{#325D88}{B}$). Of the rest, one in ten would. Given $\\color{#325D88}{B}$, how likely is $\\color{#93C54B}{A}$?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"cond-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cond-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cond-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cond-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Learning $\\color{#325D88}{B}$ throws the other 66% away, so $P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\mid \\color{#325D88}{B}) = P(\\color{#93C54B}{A} \\cap \\color{#325D88}{B})/P(\\color{#325D88}{B})$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Law of Total Probability\n",
    "\n",
    "## Librarian or Farmer? {.stage-slide #bayes-hook}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Steve is very shy and withdrawn, invariably helpful but with very little interest in people or in the world of reality. A meek and tidy soul, he has a need for order and structure, and a passion for detail.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-prior}\n",
    "<div id=\"prior-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"prior-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"prior-frag-2\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "The description never changes; the answer does — because of a number that is not in it. That number is the base rate, and the next slide goes and counts it.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Twenty Farmers for Every Librarian {.stage-slide #bayes-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-bayes}\n",
    "<div id=\"bayes-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayes-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayes-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayes-frag-3\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayes-frag-4\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "The base rate is how common each job is, before you read a word about Steve. His description fits librarians four times better. But there are twenty times more farmers, so he is still five times more likely to be one.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Formula is Just That Picture {.stage-slide #bayeq-formula}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"bayeq-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayeq-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayeq-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayeq-frag-3\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"bayeq-frag-4\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "## Move the Prior, Watch the Answer Move {.sim-slide #bayes-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 3 · the base rate does the work\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "prior <- 1 / 21 # <-- P(librarian)\n",
    "n <- 300000\n",
    "\n",
    "lib  <- runif(n) < prior\n",
    "\n",
    "# fits 40% of librarians, 10% of farmers\n",
    "fits <- runif(n) < ifelse(lib, 0.40, 0.10)\n",
    "\n",
    "c(\"P(H)\" = prior, \"P(H|E)\" = mean(lib[fits]))\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "The evidence is identical every time — only the prior changes. `1/21` is the real ratio, one librarian for every twenty farmers; at `0.50` you get 0.80. **The description never had an opinion about how common librarians are, so the base rate has to supply it.**\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Monty Hall Problem {.stage-slide #monty}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "A car sits behind one of three doors and a goat behind each of the others. You pick door 1. The host, who knows where the car is, opens door 3 on a goat and offers you the swap. Do you take it?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-monty}\n",
    "<div id=\"monty-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"monty-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"monty-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"monty-frag-3\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"monty-frag-4\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"monty-frag-5\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"monty-frag-6\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "## Combinatorics and the Factorial {.formula-slide #combinatorics}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "There are $n$ choices and you are taking $r$ of them. Two questions settle which formula you need, whether you sample with replacement and whether the order matters.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$n! = n \\times (n-1) \\times \\cdots \\times 2 \\times 1 \\qquad 0! = 1$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::::: {.three-col}\n",
    "::: {}\n",
    "[With replacement]{.col-head}\n",
    "[Order matters]{.col-sub}\n",
    "\n",
    "$$n^{\\,r}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "[Ten coin flips in a row]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "$2^{10} = 1{,}024$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {}\n",
    "[Without replacement]{.col-head}\n",
    "[Order matters]{.col-sub}\n",
    "[Permutation · $^{n}P_{r}$]{.col-term}\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "[Five cards off the top of the deck]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\begin{array}{@{}r@{\\;}c@{\\;}l@{}}\n",
    "\\dfrac{52!}{47!} & = & 52 \\times 51 \\times 50 \\times 49 \\times 48 \\\\[3pt]\n",
    " & = & 311{,}875{,}200\n",
    "\\end{array}$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {}\n",
    "[Without replacement]{.col-head}\n",
    "[Order does not matter]{.col-sub}\n",
    "[Combination · $^{n}C_{r}$]{.col-term}\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!} = \\dbinom{n}{r}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "[One hand, dealt either way]{.col-lab}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stack}\n",
    "```{=html}\n",
    "<div class=\"perm-row\"><span>K Q 2 3 7</span><span>K 2 Q 7 3</span></div>\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\dbinom{52}{5} = 2{,}598{,}960$\n",
    ":::\n",
    ":::\n",
    ":::::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.note-box}\n",
    "The last two differ by $r!$, the orders the $r$ you picked could have come in. Those 311,875,200 runs collapse to 2,598,960 hands because one hand can be dealt $5! = 120$ ways.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Probability Distributions {.hero #s04-distributions}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"4\"}\n",
    "Seminar 4 · Binomial, Poisson, exponential, normal\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Random Variables\n",
    "\n",
    "## Which Seller Do You Trust? {.stage-slide #seller-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Three sellers on the same marketplace, same item, same price. The only thing you have to go on is what previous buyers said about them. Which one do you buy from?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-seller}\n",
    "<div id=\"seller-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"seller-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"seller-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"seller-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "What is the rational way to think about the trade-off between the confidence gained from more data versus the lower absolute percentage?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Laplace's Rule of Succession {.stage-slide #laplace-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "In 1774 Laplace said not to rate a seller $k/n$. Credit one imaginary good review and one bad one, then rate them $(k+1)/(n+2)$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-seller}\n",
    "<div id=\"laplace-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"laplace-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"laplace-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"laplace-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "What assumptions are underlying this and how changing either those assumptions can change the choice you make?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Where the Binomial Comes From {.stage-slide #binom-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Every review is an independent draw at one constant unknown rate $S$. We cannot work back to it, only forwards. Suppose $S$ and see what it predicts.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"binom-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"binom-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"binom-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"binom-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "## What If the Rate Were Different? {.ojs-slide #likelihood-live}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "One guess at $S$ gives one number. Turn the dial and the top plot reshapes, while the bottom traces that number against every $S$ it could be.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof s = Inputs.range([0.50, 1.00], {value: 0.95, step: 0.01, label: \"True success rate s\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "n = 50\n",
    "k = 48\n",
    "\n",
    "// The deck's palette, so the plots match the hand-drawn stages either side.\n",
    "PAL = ({olive: \"#93C54B\", brick: \"#B94A48\", navy: \"#325D88\",\n",
    "        orange: \"#F47C3C\", soft: \"#6E7681\", dim: \"#B0B7C0\"})\n",
    "\n",
    "// Binomial pmf by recurrence: P(0) = (1-p)^n, then\n",
    "// P(j) = P(j-1) * (n-j+1)/j * p/(1-p). No factorial is ever formed, so\n",
    "// nothing overflows — the same route stage-kit.html uses.\n",
    "pmf = function (p) {\n",
    "  const q = 1 - p;\n",
    "  if (q <= 0) return Array.from({length: n + 1}, (_, i) => (i === n ? 1 : 0));\n",
    "  let v = Math.pow(q, n);\n",
    "  const out = [v];\n",
    "  for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) { v = v * ((n - j + 1) / j) * (p / q); out.push(v); }\n",
    "  return out;\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "pk = pmf(s)\n",
    "value = pk[k]\n",
    "lik = d3.range(0, 1.0005, 0.004).map(sv => ({s: sv, p: pmf(sv)[k]}))\n",
    "\n",
    "PSTYLE = ({fontSize: \"15px\", fontFamily: \"Roboto, sans-serif\",\n",
    "           color: PAL.soft, background: \"transparent\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "Plot.plot({\n",
    "  // marginTop 16, not 4: at 4 the 30% tick label has no room above the top\n",
    "  // gridline and reads as cut off. Height grows to match so the plotting\n",
    "  // area is unchanged.\n",
    "  width: 950, height: 154, marginLeft: 64, marginBottom: 24, marginTop: 16,\n",
    "  style: PSTYLE,\n",
    "  x: {domain: [-0.6, n + 0.6], ticks: d3.range(0, n + 1, 5), label: null, tickSize: 4},\n",
    "  // labelAnchor centre rotates the label down the axis; Plot's default puts\n",
    "  // it at the top, where it sits on the top tick.\n",
    "  // Both plots are pinned to 0-30% so the two heights read against the same\n",
    "  // scale. This one does exceed it once s passes about 0.97 — at s = 1 every\n",
    "  // draw is fifty ticks, so that bar is 100% — hence clip, which flattens it\n",
    "  // at the top of the frame instead of letting it paint over the equation.\n",
    "  y: {domain: [0, 0.30], ticks: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3], grid: true,\n",
    "      tickFormat: d => d3.format(\".0%\")(d), label: \"P(k | s)\",\n",
    "      labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 56},\n",
    "  marks: [\n",
    "    Plot.ruleX(d3.range(n + 1), {x: d => d, y2: d => pk[d], strokeWidth: 5,\n",
    "      stroke: d => (d === k ? PAL.olive : PAL.dim), clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: PAL.dim})\n",
    "  ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "html`<div class=\"ojs-eq\">P(<span class=\"hit\">48&#10003;</span>, <span class=\"miss\">2&#10007;</span> | s = <span class=\"rate\">${s.toFixed(2)}</span>) = <b>${value.toFixed(4)}</b></div>`\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "Plot.plot({\n",
    "  // marginTop 16, not 4: at 4 the 30% tick label has no room above the top\n",
    "  // gridline and reads as cut off. Height grows to match so the plotting\n",
    "  // area is unchanged.\n",
    "  width: 950, height: 154, marginLeft: 64, marginBottom: 24, marginTop: 16,\n",
    "  style: PSTYLE,\n",
    "  x: {domain: [0, 1], ticks: [0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, 1], label: null, tickSize: 4},\n",
    "  y: {domain: [0, 0.30], ticks: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3], grid: true,\n",
    "      tickFormat: d => d3.format(\".0%\")(d),\n",
    "      label: \"P(data | s)\", labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 56},\n",
    "  marks: [\n",
    "    Plot.line(lik, {x: \"s\", y: \"p\", stroke: PAL.navy, strokeWidth: 2.5}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleX([s], {stroke: PAL.soft, strokeDasharray: \"4 4\"}),\n",
    "    Plot.dot([{s: s, p: value}], {x: \"s\", y: \"p\", fill: PAL.olive, r: 6,\n",
    "      stroke: \"white\", strokeWidth: 1.5}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: PAL.dim})\n",
    "  ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Binomial in Standard Notation {.formula-slide #binom-formal}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Everything so far, in the notation the worksheet uses. The rate we have been calling $S$ is written $\\color{#F47C3C}{p}$; its complement is $\\color{#B94A48}{q} = 1 - \\color{#F47C3C}{p}$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$P(X = \\color{#93C54B}{k}) \\;=\\; \\binom{n}{\\color{#93C54B}{k}}\\; \\color{#F47C3C}{p}^{\\,\\color{#93C54B}{k}}\\; \\color{#B94A48}{q}^{\\,\\color{#B94A48}{n-k}} \\qquad X \\sim \\operatorname{Binomial}(n, \\color{#F47C3C}{p})$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.fragment .note}\n",
    "One review is a **Bernoulli trial**, a single attempt with two outcomes, scored $1$ for good and $0$ for bad.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.fragment}\n",
    "$$P(B_i = 1) = \\color{#F47C3C}{p}, \\quad P(B_i = 0) = \\color{#B94A48}{q}, \\quad \\operatorname{E}[B_i] = \\color{#F47C3C}{p}, \\quad \\operatorname{Var}(B_i) = \\color{#F47C3C}{p}\\color{#B94A48}{q}$$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.fragment}\n",
    "$$X \\;=\\; B_1 + B_2 + \\cdots + B_n, \\qquad B_i \\sim \\operatorname{Bernoulli}(\\color{#F47C3C}{p})$$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "Each of seller B's fifty reviews was one $B_i$. The binomial is nothing more than their sum, which is why only the count survives.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Rare Event Limit of a Binomial Distribution {.ojs-slide #pois-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Hold the average $\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} = n\\,\\color{#F47C3C}{p}$ still and push $n$ up. Each trial gets rarer, but you expect the same number of events.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof nTrials = Inputs.range([10, 400], {value: 10, step: 1, label: \"Trials n\"})\n",
    "// Floor of 10, not 5: lam runs to 8, and n < lam would ask for p > 1, which\n",
    "// the recurrence below happily turns into a 1971% gap. NB a comment must NOT\n",
    "// be the first line of an {ojs} cell: it shadows the '//|' directive and\n",
    "// Quarto then emits the whole slide as plain source, with no runtime and no\n",
    "// error anywhere.\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof lam = Inputs.range([1, 8], {value: 3, step: 0.5, label: \"Mean λ = np\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "PPAL = ({olive: \"#93C54B\", brick: \"#B94A48\", navy: \"#325D88\",\n",
    "         orange: \"#F47C3C\", soft: \"#6E7681\", dim: \"#B0B7C0\"})\n",
    "\n",
    "pp = lam / nTrials                       // the success rate, shrinking as nTrials grows\n",
    "KMAX = Math.max(12, Math.ceil(lam + 4 * Math.sqrt(lam)))\n",
    "ks = d3.range(0, KMAX + 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "// Binomial(nTrials, pp) by the same recurrence used everywhere else in this deck\n",
    "binom = {\n",
    "  const q = 1 - pp;\n",
    "  if (q <= 0) return ks.map(k => (k === nTrials ? 1 : 0));\n",
    "  let v = Math.pow(q, nTrials);\n",
    "  const out = [v];\n",
    "  const top = Math.min(nTrials, KMAX);   // KMAX <= 20; no point walking to 20,000\n",
    "  for (let j = 1; j <= top; j++) { v = v * ((nTrials - j + 1) / j) * (pp / q); out.push(v); }\n",
    "  return ks.map(k => (k <= nTrials ? out[k] : 0));\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "// Poisson(lam): P(0) = e^-lam, then P(k) = P(k-1) * lam / k\n",
    "pois = {\n",
    "  const out = []; let v = Math.exp(-lam);\n",
    "  for (let k = 0; k <= KMAX; k++) { out.push(v); v = v * lam / (k + 1); }\n",
    "  return out;\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "gap = d3.max(ks, k => Math.abs(binom[k] - pois[k]))\n",
    "YMAX = Math.max(d3.max(binom), d3.max(pois)) * 1.18\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "Plot.plot({\n",
    "  width: 950, height: 205, marginLeft: 64, marginBottom: 26, marginTop: 16,\n",
    "  style: {fontSize: \"15px\", fontFamily: \"Roboto, sans-serif\",\n",
    "          color: PPAL.soft, background: \"transparent\"},\n",
    "  x: {domain: [-0.6, KMAX + 0.6], label: null, tickSize: 4},\n",
    "  y: {domain: [0, YMAX], grid: true, tickFormat: d => d3.format(\".0%\")(d),\n",
    "      label: \"P(k)\", labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 56},\n",
    "  marks: [\n",
    "    Plot.ruleX(ks, {x: d => d, y2: d => binom[d], strokeWidth: 9,\n",
    "      stroke: PPAL.dim, clip: true}),\n",
    "    // \"step\" is Plot's midpoint step (d3.curveStep); \"step-mid\" is not a\n",
    "    // curve name and throws, taking the whole plot cell with it.\n",
    "    Plot.line(ks, {x: d => d, y: d => pois[d], stroke: PPAL.brick,\n",
    "      strokeWidth: 2, curve: \"step\", clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.dot(ks, {x: d => d, y: d => pois[d], fill: PPAL.brick, r: 3.2,\n",
    "      clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: PPAL.dim})\n",
    "  ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "html`<div class=\"ojs-eq\">Binomial(${nTrials}, <span class=\"rate\">${pp.toFixed(4)}</span>) &nbsp;→&nbsp; <span class=\"miss\">Poisson(${lam.toFixed(1)})</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; gap <b>${(100*gap).toFixed(2)}%</b></div>`\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Watch what is lost. Push $n$ up and the shape stops revealing $n$ or $\\color{#F47C3C}{p}$, only their product. That is why the Poisson has *one* parameter.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Poisson in Standard Notation {.formula-slide #pois-formal}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Put $\\color{#F47C3C}{p} = \\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}/n$ into the binomial and let $n$ run away.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$P(X = \\color{#93C54B}{k}) \\;=\\; \\binom{n}{\\color{#93C54B}{k}}\\big(\\tfrac{\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}}{n}\\big)^{\\color{#93C54B}{k}}\\big(1-\\tfrac{\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}}{n}\\big)^{n-\\color{#93C54B}{k}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.deriv-arrow .fragment}\n",
    "[↓]{.deriv-glyph} [Group the factors by whether they still mention $n$]{.deriv-note}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$=\\;\\frac{\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}^{\\,\\color{#93C54B}{k}}}{\\color{#93C54B}{k}!}\\;\\cdot\\;\\frac{n(n-1)\\cdots(n-\\color{#93C54B}{k}+1)}{n^{\\color{#93C54B}{k}}}\\;\\cdot\\;\\big(1-\\tfrac{\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}}{n}\\big)^{n}\\;\\cdot\\;\\big(1-\\tfrac{\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}}{n}\\big)^{-\\color{#93C54B}{k}}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.deriv-arrow .fragment}\n",
    "[↓]{.deriv-glyph} [As $n \\to \\infty$ those three go to $1$, to $e^{-\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}}$, and to $1$]{.deriv-note}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$P(X = \\color{#93C54B}{k}) \\;=\\; \\frac{\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}^{\\,\\color{#93C54B}{k}}\\, e^{-\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}}}{\\color{#93C54B}{k}!} \\qquad X \\sim \\operatorname{Poisson}(\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda})$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "Only the middle factor did any real work, since $(1-\\lambda/n)^{n}$ *is* the definition of $e^{-\\lambda}$. One parameter now does the job of two, and $\\operatorname{E}[X] = \\operatorname{Var}(X) = \\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Check the approximation {.sim-slide #pois-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 4 · exact vs approximate\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Three mistakes per 10,000 transactions; 10,000 audited. What is the chance of four or more? Compute it both ways.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "n <- 10000\n",
    "p <- 0.0003   # <-- keep n * p = 3\n",
    "\n",
    "binomial <- 1 - pbinom(3, n, p)\n",
    "poisson <- 1 - ppois(3, n * p)\n",
    "\n",
    "c(binomial, poisson)\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "Both read `0.3527681`, so every digit R prints agrees and the approximation costs you nothing here. Now set `n <- 20; p <- 0.15` (still $np = 3$) and watch it open up. **That** is what \"large $n$, small $p$\" is protecting you from.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Time Between Poisson Events {.ojs-slide #expo-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Count arrivals, or time the wait. The bridge is the $\\color{#93C54B}{k = 0}$ bar.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof lamE = Inputs.range([0.5, 4], {value: 2, step: 0.5, label: \"Rate λ per minute\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof winT = Inputs.range([0.1, 2.5], {value: 1, step: 0.05, label: \"Window t minutes\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "muE = lamE * winT                     // arrivals expected in the window\n",
    "KMAXE = Math.max(8, Math.ceil(muE + 4 * Math.sqrt(muE)))\n",
    "ksE = d3.range(0, KMAXE + 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "// Poisson(muE) by the deck's usual recurrence: P(0) = e^-mu, P(k) = P(k-1) mu/k\n",
    "poisE = {\n",
    "  const out = []; let v = Math.exp(-muE);\n",
    "  for (let k = 0; k <= KMAXE; k++) { out.push(v); v = v * muE / (k + 1); }\n",
    "  return out;\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "// The whole point of the slide: the k = 0 bar IS the exponential's survival\n",
    "// curve. P(no arrival in [0, t]) = e^(-lambda t) = P(wait > t).\n",
    "p0E = Math.exp(-muE)\n",
    "TE = 3                                // minutes on the bottom axis\n",
    "densE = t => lamE * Math.exp(-lamE * t)\n",
    "gridE = d3.range(0, 241).map(i => TE * i / 240)\n",
    "tailE = d3.range(0, 161).map(i => winT + (TE - winT) * i / 160)\n",
    "YPOIS = d3.max(poisE) * 1.18\n",
    "YDENS = lamE * 1.12\n",
    "ESTYLE = ({fontSize: \"15px\", fontFamily: \"Roboto, sans-serif\",\n",
    "           color: PPAL.soft, background: \"transparent\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "Plot.plot({\n",
    "  width: 950, height: 154, marginLeft: 64, marginBottom: 24, marginTop: 16,\n",
    "  style: ESTYLE,\n",
    "  x: {domain: [-0.6, KMAXE + 0.6], label: null, tickSize: 4},\n",
    "  y: {domain: [0, YPOIS], grid: true, tickFormat: d => d3.format(\".0%\")(d),\n",
    "      label: \"P(k arrivals in t)\", labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 56},\n",
    "  marks: [\n",
    "    Plot.ruleX(ksE, {x: d => d, y2: d => poisE[d], strokeWidth: 7,\n",
    "      stroke: d => (d === 0 ? PPAL.olive : PPAL.dim), clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: PPAL.dim})\n",
    "  ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "html`<div class=\"ojs-eq\">P(<span class=\"hit\">0 arrivals</span> in ${winT.toFixed(2)} min) &nbsp;=&nbsp; <b>${p0E.toFixed(4)}</b> &nbsp;=&nbsp; P(<span class=\"hit\">wait &gt; ${winT.toFixed(2)}</span>) &nbsp;·&nbsp; <span class=\"rate\">λ = ${lamE.toFixed(1)}</span></div>`\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "Plot.plot({\n",
    "  width: 950, height: 154, marginLeft: 64, marginBottom: 24, marginTop: 16,\n",
    "  style: ESTYLE,\n",
    "  x: {domain: [0, TE], label: null, tickSize: 4},\n",
    "  y: {domain: [0, YDENS], grid: true, tickFormat: d => d3.format(\".1f\")(d),\n",
    "      label: \"density of the wait\", labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 56},\n",
    "  marks: [\n",
    "    // This olive area is the same number as the olive bar above it.\n",
    "    Plot.areaY(tailE, {x: t => t, y: densE, fill: PPAL.olive,\n",
    "      fillOpacity: 0.3, clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.line(gridE, {x: t => t, y: densE, stroke: PPAL.brick,\n",
    "      strokeWidth: 2.5, clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleX([winT], {stroke: PPAL.soft, strokeDasharray: \"4 4\"}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: PPAL.dim})\n",
    "  ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Exponential in Standard Notation {.formula-slide #expo-formal}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "The wait beats $t$ exactly when nothing arrives in $[0, t]$, so read the Poisson at $\\color{#93C54B}{k = 0}$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$P(T > t) \\;=\\; P\\big(N(t) = \\color{#93C54B}{0}\\big), \\qquad N(t) \\sim \\operatorname{Poisson}(\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} t)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.deriv-arrow .fragment}\n",
    "[↓]{.deriv-glyph} [Put $\\color{#93C54B}{k = 0}$ into the Poisson formula]{.deriv-note}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$=\\;\\frac{(\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} t)^{\\color{#93C54B}{0}}\\, e^{-\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} t}}{\\color{#93C54B}{0}!} \\;=\\; e^{-\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} t}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.deriv-arrow .fragment}\n",
    "[↓]{.deriv-glyph} [That is the survival curve, so the density is the slope of $1 - e^{-\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} t}$]{.deriv-note}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$f(t) \\;=\\; \\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}\\, e^{-\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} t} \\qquad T \\sim \\operatorname{Exponential}(\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda})$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "The same $\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}$, read the other way round. $\\operatorname{E}[T] = 1/\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda}$ is the average gap between arrivals.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Summary: Three Questions, Three Distributions {.qcard-slide #dist-questions}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Each one answers exactly one question.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.qcard .qcard-binom .fragment}\n",
    "[$\\operatorname{Binomial}(n, p)$]{.qcard-name} [How many successes in a fixed number of attempts?]{.qcard-q}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.qcard .qcard-pois .fragment}\n",
    "[$\\operatorname{Poisson}(\\lambda)$]{.qcard-name} [How many events happen in a fixed amount of time?]{.qcard-q}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.qcard .qcard-expo .fragment}\n",
    "[$\\operatorname{Exponential}(\\lambda)$]{.qcard-name} [How long until the next event?]{.qcard-q}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "The first two count and the third measures, so only the third has no $n$ and no $k$ in it.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Limit of a Binomial Distribution for Large n {.ojs-slide #normal-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Hold $\\color{#F47C3C}{p}$ still and push $n$ up, and the bars settle onto a bell.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof nBig = Inputs.range([5, 200], {value: 10, step: 1, label: \"Trials n\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "viewof pBig = Inputs.range([0.05, 0.95], {value: 0.35, step: 0.05, label: \"Success rate p\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "muB = nBig * pBig\n",
    "sdB = Math.sqrt(nBig * pBig * (1 - pBig))\n",
    "kLo = Math.max(0, Math.floor(muB - 4 * sdB))\n",
    "kHi = Math.min(nBig, Math.ceil(muB + 4 * sdB))\n",
    "ksB = d3.range(kLo, kHi + 1)\n",
    "\n",
    "// Binomial(nBig, pBig) by the deck's usual recurrence, walked only as far as\n",
    "// the plot shows: P(0) = (1-p)^n, P(j) = P(j-1) (n-j+1)/j p/(1-p).\n",
    "binomB = {\n",
    "  const q = 1 - pBig;\n",
    "  let v = Math.pow(q, nBig);\n",
    "  const out = [v];\n",
    "  for (let j = 1; j <= kHi; j++) { v = v * ((nBig - j + 1) / j) * (pBig / q); out.push(v); }\n",
    "  return out;\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "// De Moivre's curve: N(np, np(1-p)), read as a density at each whole k.\n",
    "normB = x => Math.exp(-((x - muB) ** 2) / (2 * sdB * sdB)) / (sdB * Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI))\n",
    "gapB = d3.max(ksB, k => Math.abs(binomB[k] - normB(k)))\n",
    "YMAXB = d3.max(ksB, k => Math.max(binomB[k], normB(k))) * 1.18\n",
    "BWB = Math.max(1.5, Math.min(9, 880 / (kHi - kLo + 1) - 1))\n",
    "gridB = d3.range(0, 241).map(i => kLo + (kHi - kLo) * i / 240)\n",
    "BSTYLE = ({fontSize: \"15px\", fontFamily: \"Roboto, sans-serif\",\n",
    "           color: PPAL.soft, background: \"transparent\"})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "Plot.plot({\n",
    "  width: 950, height: 205, marginLeft: 64, marginBottom: 26, marginTop: 16,\n",
    "  style: BSTYLE,\n",
    "  x: {domain: [kLo - 0.6, kHi + 0.6], label: null, tickSize: 4},\n",
    "  y: {domain: [0, YMAXB], grid: true, tickFormat: d => d3.format(\".0%\")(d),\n",
    "      label: \"P(k)\", labelAnchor: \"center\", labelOffset: 56},\n",
    "  marks: [\n",
    "    Plot.ruleX(ksB, {x: d => d, y2: d => binomB[d], strokeWidth: BWB,\n",
    "      stroke: PPAL.dim, clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.line(gridB, {x: d => d, y: normB, stroke: PPAL.navy,\n",
    "      strokeWidth: 2.5, clip: true}),\n",
    "    Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: PPAL.dim})\n",
    "  ]\n",
    "})\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "```{ojs}\n",
    "//| echo: false\n",
    "html`<div class=\"ojs-eq\">Binomial(${nBig}, <span class=\"rate\">${pBig.toFixed(2)}</span>) &nbsp;→&nbsp; <span class=\"miss\">N(${muB.toFixed(1)}, ${(sdB*sdB).toFixed(1)})</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; gap <b>${(100*gapB).toFixed(2)}%</b></div>`\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Two limits come out of one distribution. Rare events with $\\color{#F47C3C}{\\lambda} = n\\color{#F47C3C}{p}$ held still give the Poisson, plenty of events with $\\color{#F47C3C}{p}$ held still give the normal.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why the normal turns up at all {.stage-slide #clt-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"clt-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"clt-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"clt-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"clt-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "The population on the left never changes and never becomes normal; the **mean** does, and it narrows at the rate $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$. Both panels share one axis, so the narrowing is real, not a rescaling.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Break the bell on purpose {.sim-slide #clt-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 4 · the CLT, stress-tested\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-plot}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "#| fig-width: 5.7\n",
    "#| fig-height: 4.3\n",
    "#| out-width: \"100%\"\n",
    "n <- 5             # <-- draws per average\n",
    "pop <- function(k) rexp(k)^2   # no bell at all\n",
    "\n",
    "xbar <- replicate(4000, mean(pop(n)))\n",
    "hist(xbar, breaks = 40, col = \"#CBD9E6\",\n",
    "     border = \"white\", main = \"\",\n",
    "     xlab = paste(\"mean of\", n, \"draws\"))\n",
    "\n",
    "round(c(sd_obs  = sd(xbar),\n",
    "        se_pred = sd(pop(1e5)) / sqrt(n)), 3)\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "`n <- 1` is the raw population, violently skewed; by `n <- 30` it is nearly symmetric and `sd_obs` tracks $\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}$. **The CLT is a claim about the mean, and it survives a parent this ugly.**\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## One table for every normal {.stage-slide #zstd-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"zstd-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"zstd-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"zstd-frag-2\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Subtracting $\\mu$ moves the curve; dividing by $\\sigma$ squeezes it, so it grows *taller* — area is always 1. The shaded probability never moves: standardising changes the ruler, not the answer.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Functions of a Random Variable {.formula-slide #funcrv}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "For $\\color{#93C54B}{Y} = g(X)$, work through the *CDF* and never the density. Ask which values of $X$ make $\\color{#93C54B}{Y}$ small enough.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$F_{\\color{#93C54B}{Y}}(y) \\;=\\; P(a X + b \\le y) \\;=\\; P\\big(X \\le \\tfrac{y-b}{a}\\big)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.deriv-arrow .fragment}\n",
    "[↓]{.deriv-glyph} [The right-hand side is just $X$'s own CDF, read at a shifted point]{.deriv-note}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$F_{\\color{#93C54B}{Y}}(y) \\;=\\; F_X\\big(\\tfrac{y-b}{a}\\big)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.deriv-arrow .fragment}\n",
    "[↓]{.deriv-glyph} [Differentiate in $y$; the chain rule brings down the $1/a$]{.deriv-note}\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "$$f_{\\color{#93C54B}{Y}}(y) \\;=\\; \\tfrac{1}{|a|}\\, f_X\\big(\\tfrac{y-b}{a}\\big) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\color{#93C54B}{Y} \\sim \\operatorname{N}\\big(a\\mu + b,\\; a^{2}\\sigma^{2}\\big)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer .fragment}\n",
    "Standardising is this with $a = 1/\\sigma$ and $b = -\\mu/\\sigma$, which is why one table serves every normal. A *non-linear* $g$ leaves the family, so $X \\sim \\operatorname{N}(0,1)$ makes $\\color{#93C54B}{Y} = X^{2}$ chi-squared.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Estimation {.hero #s05-estimation}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"5\"}\n",
    "Seminar 5 · Confidence intervals\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "The interval is random. The truth is not.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede}\n",
    "The formula $\\bar x \\pm z\\,\\sigma/\\sqrt n$ looks like it was handed down. It was not — it is one geometric move, performed on the distribution of $\\bar x$, and once you have seen the move the algebra is a transcript of it.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The move that builds the interval {.stage-slide #ciflip-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"ciflip-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"ciflip-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"ciflip-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"ciflip-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Nothing new is built at the flip: the bracket is the *same width*, slid from $\\mu$ to $\\bar x$, so \"$\\bar x$ within 5.88 of $\\mu$\" and \"$\\mu$ within 5.88 of $\\bar x$\" are one statement, and the second is the interval.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Four parts, one interval {.stage-slide #cieq-formula}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"cieq-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cieq-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cieq-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cieq-frag-3\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"cieq-frag-4\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Only $\\bar x$ came from your sample; $z$ you chose, $\\sigma$ and $n$ you knew, so the half-width 5.88 was fixed *before you collected anything*: every interval on the next slide is exactly the same length.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## What is actually random here? {.stage-slide #ci-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-ci}\n",
    "<div id=\"ci-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    ":::: {.claim-pair}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.claim-cell .claim-wrong}\n",
    "[Tempting]{.claim-tag}\n",
    "\n",
    "\"There is a 95% probability that $\\mu$ lies in **my** interval.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "$\\mu$ is a fixed number. It is either in there or it is not — no probability left to speak of.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.claim-cell .claim-right}\n",
    "[Correct]{.claim-tag}\n",
    "\n",
    "\"95% of intervals **built this way** contain $\\mu$.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "The randomness is in the sampling, so it lives in the interval's endpoints — never in $\\mu$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Check the 95% yourself {.sim-slide #ci-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 5 · coverage\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-plot}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "#| fig-width: 5.7\n",
    "#| fig-height: 4.3\n",
    "#| out-width: \"100%\"\n",
    "mu <- 100; sigma <- 15; reps <- 100  # the truth\n",
    "n <- 25; conf <- 0.95                # <-- knobs\n",
    "se   <- sigma / sqrt(n)\n",
    "half <- qnorm(1 - (1 - conf)/2) * se\n",
    "xbar <- replicate(reps, mean(rnorm(n,mu,sigma)))\n",
    "hit  <- abs(xbar - mu) <= half\n",
    "plot(NA, ylim = c(1, reps), xlim=mu+c(-4,4)*se,\n",
    "     xlab = \"interval\", ylab = \"sample\", las=1)\n",
    "abline(v = mu, lty = 2, lwd=2, col = \"#325D88\")\n",
    "segments(xbar-half, 1:reps, xbar+half, 1:reps,\n",
    "        col = ifelse(hit, \"#9FB6CC\", \"#B94A48\"))\n",
    "mean(hit)\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "Run it a few times — the red misses wander around 5, they are not pinned to it. Then set `conf <- 0.80`: shorter intervals, more misses. **Precision is bought with coverage.**\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why the worksheet makes you switch to $t$ {.sim-slide #ci-t-vs-z}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 5 · $\\sigma$ known vs unknown\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "n <- 5            # <-- try 5, then 25, then 100\n",
    "mu <- 100; sigma <- 15; reps <- 2000\n",
    "# same sample sd; only the cut-off differs\n",
    "z_rule <- function(x) qnorm(.975)*sd(x)/sqrt(n)\n",
    "t_rule <- function(x) qt(.975,n-1)*sd(x)/sqrt(n)\n",
    "cover <- function(rule) mean(replicate(reps, {\n",
    "  x <- rnorm(n, mu, sigma)\n",
    "  abs(mean(x) - mu) <= rule(x)\n",
    "}))\n",
    "round(c(used_z = cover(z_rule),\n",
    "        used_t = cover(t_rule)), 3)\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "At `n <- 5` the $z$ rule lands near 87%, not 95% — you paid for confidence you never received. The $t$ correction buys it back. Push `n` to 100 and the two rules converge, which is why the distinction stops mattering for large samples.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Hypothesis Testing {.hero #s06-testing}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"6\"}\n",
    "Seminar 6 · p-values and error rates\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "A p-value answers one question, and it is not the one you want.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede}\n",
    "It does not tell you the probability that $H_0$ is true. It tells you how ordinary your sample would look in a world where $H_0$ *is* true — which is a question about the world, not about your hypothesis.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Build the null world, then look {.stage-slide #pval-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"pval-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"pval-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"pval-frag-2\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"pval-frag-3\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Nothing here required a table. The p-value is a counting exercise: of all the samples $H_0$ would produce, what share are at least as extreme as ours?\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## What $\\alpha$ actually buys {.sim-slide #alpha-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 6 · Type I error\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede-min}\n",
    "Below, $H_0$ is **true** in every single one of the 5,000 worlds. Every rejection is therefore a false alarm — so the rejection rate should come out at $\\alpha$.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "alpha <- 0.05   # <-- your significance level\n",
    "reps  <- 5000\n",
    "n     <- 30\n",
    "reject <- replicate(reps, {\n",
    "  x <- rnorm(n, 100, 15)   # H0 is TRUE\n",
    "  t <- (mean(x) - 100) / (sd(x)/sqrt(n))\n",
    "  abs(t) > qt(1 - alpha/2, n - 1)\n",
    "})\n",
    "mean(reject)   # how often we cried wolf\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "You get back roughly `alpha`. Set it to `0.01` and the false-alarm rate follows. **$\\alpha$ is not a truth threshold — it is the false-alarm rate you have agreed to live with.**\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## \"Fail to reject\" is not \"accept\" {.sim-slide #power-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 6 · the other error · H₀ false, true mean 105\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    ":::: {.claim-pair}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.claim-cell .claim-wrong}\n",
    "[Wrong]{.claim-tag}\n",
    "\n",
    "\"We failed to reject, so the means are equal.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence — a small sample fails to reject almost everything.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.claim-cell .claim-right}\n",
    "[Right]{.claim-tag}\n",
    "\n",
    "\"We had too little evidence to rule equality out.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Whether that is reassuring depends entirely on how much power you had.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "n <- 10   # <-- try 10, then 50, then 200\n",
    "mean(replicate(4000, {\n",
    "  x <- rnorm(n, mean = 105, sd = 15)\n",
    "  t <- (mean(x) - 100) / (sd(x)/sqrt(n))\n",
    "  abs(t) > qt(0.975, n - 1)\n",
    "}))\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "At `n <- 10` you detect a real effect about **16%** of the time. Failing to reject told you nothing — the test could barely see. At `n <- 200` it is near certain. **\"No significant difference\" is only informative once you know the power.**\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Comparing Two Populations {.hero #s07-two-populations}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"7\"}\n",
    "Seminar 7 · Paired vs independent\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "The design decides the test, and the test decides the answer.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede}\n",
    "The worksheet gives you two-sample tests and paired tests as separate recipes. They are not interchangeable — run the wrong one on the same numbers and a real effect can vanish completely.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The same data, twice {.stage-slide #pair-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"pair-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"pair-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"pair-frag-2\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "The left picture is honest and useless: firms differ hugely, and that between-firm spread drowns the effect. Pairing subtracts it away; every firm becomes its own control.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Watch the signal appear {.sim-slide #pair-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 7 · same numbers, two tests\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "before <- c(3, 15, 8, 19, 5, 12, 17, 7,\n",
    "            14, 10, 6, 16, 9, 13, 11)\n",
    "after  <- c(1, 13, 5, 17, 4, 9, 16, 4,\n",
    "            12, 7, 5, 13, 7, 10, 9)\n",
    "ind <- t.test(after, before, var.equal = TRUE)\n",
    "par <- t.test(after, before, paired = TRUE)\n",
    "row <- function(r) c(t = unname(r$statistic),\n",
    "                     p = r$p.value)\n",
    "round(rbind(independent = row(ind),\n",
    "            paired      = row(par)), 4)\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "Identical numbers, identical difference in means of $-2.2$ — but the independent test cannot see it and the paired test finds it overwhelming. **The information was in which rows belong together, and the independent test throws that away.**\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "# Correlation and Regression {.hero #s08-regression}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sem-mark data-sem=\"8\"}\n",
    "Seminar 8 · OLS and what it hides\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "\"Least squares\" is not a metaphor.\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.lede}\n",
    "The formulas on the worksheet fall out of one instruction: draw a square on every residual, then pick the line that makes the total area as small as possible. Everything else — $R^2$, the slope's standard error — is bookkeeping on top of that.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The squares in least squares {.stage-slide #ls-intuition}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.stage-wrap .stage-wrap-std}\n",
    "<div id=\"ls-stage\" class=\"stage\"></div>\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"ls-frag-1\"}\n",
    "[]{.fragment .fi-frag id=\"ls-frag-2\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-footer}\n",
    "Squares, not distances — which is why one far-off point can drag the whole line toward it. Doubling a residual quadruples its say in where the line goes.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## Your formula, and `lm()` {.sim-slide #ols-sim}\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.slide-kicker}\n",
    "Seminar 8 · $\\hat\\beta_1 = S_{xy}/S_{xx}$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.sim-card .sim-text}\n",
    "```{webr-r}\n",
    "x <- 1:9\n",
    "y <- c(3.1,4.4,4.2,6.1,6.6,8.3,8.1,9.9,10.6)\n",
    "\n",
    "# the worksheet formula, by hand\n",
    "b1 <- sum((x - mean(x)) * (y - mean(y))) /\n",
    "      sum((x - mean(x))^2)\n",
    "b0 <- mean(y) - b1 * mean(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "rbind(by_hand = c(b0, b1),\n",
    "      lm      = coef(lm(y ~ x)))\n",
    "```\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {.try-this}\n",
    "Identical to the last decimal. `lm()` is not doing anything you cannot do by hand — it is doing exactly this, faster.\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "## The variable you left out {.sim-slide #ovb-sim}\n",
